{"id": "2025-03-18-starliner-crew-return-crew-9", "result": "corrected", "evidence": [{"doc": 2, "quote": "Wilmore and Williams originally planned to spend 10 days in space, but eventually spent more than nine months in orbit while waiting for a ride home on a Dragon spacecraft built by Boeing's competitor SpaceX. (SpaceX has its own multi-billion dollar contract to fly NASA astronauts to and from the ISS, and has done so regularly since 2020.) The Starliner astronauts returned to Earth in March 2025, more than six months after the Starliner capsule landed on its own in September 2024."}], "fetched": [{"doc": 1, "url": "https://www.space.com/space-exploration/human-spaceflight/boeing-starliner-wont-carry-nasa-astronauts-next-flight", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 16784, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T15:56:58+00:00", "sha256": "4c5a2ac8bb31b438f2ab535f4840f9c358f0242c6265a30c8aa9705be04f3ab8"}, {"doc": 2, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20260101183040id_/https://www.space.com/space-exploration/human-spaceflight/boeing-starliner-wont-carry-nasa-astronauts-next-flight", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 15382, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T15:57:35+00:00", "sha256": "e5b553a51bb5778675467d2fc687da3e861830d57452f0450633dbf11bedbe2d"}]}
